{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "placements_completed",
      "description": "Number of completed placements of the instructed cube on the target. Initialize to 0. Increment once at the first cycle showing the gripper opened after lowering the held cube onto the target, not at the cycle commanding that lowering. Keep the count through withdrawal and subsequent regrasping. A cube merely remaining on the target does not increment it. The initial placement counts toward the instructed total. e.g. 2",
      "value_type": "count"
    },
    {
      "name": "milestone",
      "description": "Most recently observed manipulation milestone, using exactly these values: start, cube_hover, cube_grasped, cube_lifted, target_hover, cube_released, release_retracted, button_hover, button_closed, button_pressed, button_retracted. Initialize to start. Update on the cycle showing completion of the corresponding motion or gripper change, never when its command is issued. cube_hover means the open hand has completed its dedicated alignment above the cube; cube_grasped means fingers have closed around it at pickup height; cube_lifted means it has been raised to transport height; target_hover means the subsequent target alignment has completed; cube_released means the hand has opened at placement height; release_retracted means the open hand has withdrawn to transport height. The button values respectively mean arrival above the button, empty gripper closure there, downward contact, and withdrawal. Retain the value if no motion or relevant change occurred. e.g. \"cube_lifted\"",
      "value_type": "text"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "placements_completed",
      "description": "Initialize to 0 with empty memory. Increment once when this cycle shows the previously held instructed cube lowered onto the target and the fingers now opened, with incoming milestone cube_lifted or target_hover. This is the observed release, not the command to release. Do not increment on withdrawal, on seeing the cube still on the target, or on a zero-step action. Otherwise carry the count unchanged. The initial placement counts toward the instructed total. e.g. 2"
    },
    {
      "name": "milestone",
      "description": "Initialize to start. Record only achieved events visible in this cycle's motion frames and current state. From start or release_retracted, completion of open-hand alignment above the instructed cube gives cube_hover. Closure around that cube at low height gives cube_grasped; raising it to about 0.15 m gives cube_lifted. A subsequent high target-alignment motion gives target_hover. From cube_lifted or target_hover, lowering and opening over the target gives cube_released; the subsequent open-hand ascent gives release_retracted. Once the placement count has reached the requested total, arrival above the button gives button_hover; closure of the empty fingers there gives button_closed; descent into button contact gives button_pressed; ascent after contact gives button_retracted. Use incoming milestone to distinguish a pickup alignment from withdrawal and a target alignment from the initial lift, even when their endpoints are nearly identical. Retain the incoming value if no relevant event occurred, including explicitly zero-step actions. e.g. \"cube_lifted\""
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "Read the instructed color and total N from the goal. N is the total number of placements, including the first, not N additional repetitions. Use the observed milestone and updated placement count for the following rules. Estimate world positions from calibrated front and wrist images: identify the instructed cube, purple target-ring center, and gray button on its white base. Use table-plane projection for the target and account for cube height when locating its center. Refine with the wrist view; do not confuse its image center with the TCP projection. Never substitute another colored cube. Orient the hand downward, roll approximately -3.1416 and pitch 0, with yaw aligned to the cube's horizontal edges for grasping. Preserve that orientation during carrying and release.\n\nAt start, move with open fingers above the instructed cube's current center at z=0.15 m. At release_retracted with placements_completed < N, similarly make a dedicated open-hand alignment at z=0.15 m above that same cube, now on the target; do not return to its original location. At cube_hover, descend to the cube center's x,y at z=0.02 m and close. At cube_grasped, raise the cube vertically to z=0.15 m with fingers closed, using the observed cube/grasp center for x,y.\n\nAt cube_lifted, move closed to the target center at z=0.15 m before lowering. This alignment is needed even for repeated placements with only a few millimeters of displacement. If the target-hover waypoint differs from the current TCP position by less than 2 mm, omit that redundant motion and lower directly. At target_hover, lower at the target center to commanded z=0.005 m and open. Contact may leave the actual TCP higher than the commanded height; do not repeatedly force it down. At cube_released, withdraw vertically above the target to z=0.15 m, keeping fingers open. Always withdraw after the final placement as well.\n\nAt release_retracted with placements_completed >= N, move above the button center at z=0.15 m with open fingers and orientation [-3.1416,0,0]. At button_hover, close the empty gripper while maintaining alignment and approximately this height. At button_closed, descend at the button center to commanded z=0.0075 m with fingers closed. At button_pressed, retract to z=0.15 m with fingers closed. Contact commonly stops the TCP near z=0.025 m; treat observed button contact as the press rather than demanding the commanded depth be reached.\n\nEvery waypoint must differ from the current TCP position by at least 2 mm. For a gripper-only change or otherwise necessary near-identical hover alignment, add a small upward displacement, about 3–5 mm, while keeping the intended x,y. Do not advance memory because a waypoint was merely requested. Allow ordinary tracking error when recognizing completed motions.\n\nIf an unexpected state occurs, use visible evidence rather than inventing progress: if a grasp failed, reopen and realign above the instructed cube; if holding it below transport height, lift before lateral travel; if placement or button contact is occluded, withdraw safely to reveal the result without incrementing the count speculatively. Re-estimate positions from the newly exposed scene and resume the applicable rule. After button_retracted, do not restart cube manipulation; if execution continues and the button was not visibly activated, realign above it and retry the press. Do not wait without a specific observation or physical event to resolve."
}
